\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Charged invariant tensors and multiplicity spaces}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{(R,\rho)} be a specified finite-dimensional continuous
unitary representation of the [residual group](fdip-0089)
#{K} over #{\mathbb C}. Its complex linear dual #{R^*} carries
#{(\rho^*(g)\lambda)(v)=\lambda(\rho(g)^{-1}v)}.
The \newvocab{charged physical space} for this charge datum is
##{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{phys}}(R)
 =(\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}\otimes R^*)^K,}
where #{g} acts as #{R_g\otimes\rho^*(g)}. Since #{R} is
finite dimensional, the Hilbert tensor product here is a finite
orthogonal sum of copies of #{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}}.}

\p{There is a canonical unitary identification
##{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{phys}}(R)
 \cong\operatorname{Hom}_K(R,\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}})
 =:\mathcal M_R.}
Here #{\operatorname{Hom}_K} consists of the linear maps #{F}
satisfying #{R_gF=F\rho(g)} for every #{g}; its inner product is
#{\langle F,G\rangle_{\mathrm{HS}}=\operatorname{Tr}(F^*G)},
linear in the second argument. The [transfer operator](fdip-0080)
#{T\otimes I} preserves charged physical states and corresponds
to #{F\mapsto TF}.}

\p{Suppose further that #{R} is irreducible and put #{d=\dim R}.
Its [isotypic component](fdip-004V) in this complex Hilbert setting
is #{\mathcal H_R}, defined as the
closed span of all images #{F(R)} with #{F\in\mathcal M_R}.
Then
##{\begin{aligned}
R\otimes\mathcal M_R&\longrightarrow\mathcal H_R,\\
v\otimes F&\longmapsto\sqrt d\,F(v)
\end{aligned}}
is a unitary representation isomorphism, with trivial #{K}-action
on #{\mathcal M_R}. The charged physical space is
#{\mathcal M_R}, whereas #{\mathcal H_R} includes the additional
factor #{R}. In finite dimension, their dimensions are respectively
#{\dim\mathcal M_R} and #{d\dim\mathcal M_R}.}

\proof{
\p{For an elementary tensor define
##{\Phi(x\otimes\lambda)(v)=\lambda(v)x.}
If #{e_1,\ldots,e_d} is an orthonormal basis of #{R} with dual
basis #{e_1^*,\ldots,e_d^*}, the inverse is
##{F\longmapsto\sum_{i=1}^dF(e_i)\otimes e_i^*.}
Evaluating on the basis proves both inverse identities. The map
#{\Phi} is basis independent, so its inverse is as well. Its norm
is the [Hilbert–Schmidt norm](fdip-007T)
#{\|F\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2=\sum_i\|F(e_i)\|^2}, proving that
#{\Phi} is unitary.}

\p{Under #{\Phi}, the tensor action becomes
##{F\longmapsto R_gF\rho(g)^{-1}.}
Indeed, apply both sides to #{v} on an elementary tensor. Its
fixed points are exactly the intertwiners, which proves the charged
identification. The invariant subspace is closed, so #{\mathcal M_R}
is a Hilbert space. Since #{TR_g=R_gT}, postcomposition by #{T}
preserves intertwiners and corresponds to #{T\otimes I}.}

\p{For irreducible #{R}, unitarity and the intertwining identities
show that #{F^*G:R\to R} commutes with #{\rho(K)}.
Complex Schur's lemma gives
##{F^*G=\frac{\operatorname{Tr}(F^*G)}d I_R
 =\frac{\langle F,G\rangle_{\mathrm{HS}}}d I_R.}
One elementary proof of the lemma chooses an eigenvalue of a
commuting complex endomorphism: its nonzero invariant eigenspace
must be all of an irreducible representation. Therefore
##{\langle F(v),G(w)\rangle
 =\frac1d\langle v,w\rangle
       \langle F,G\rangle_{\mathrm{HS}}.}
Extending by linearity in finite sums proves that #{\sqrt d}
times evaluation is isometric. Its range is closed, and contains
every #{F(R)}; by the definition of #{\mathcal H_R}, it is onto.
The intertwining identity makes it equivariant. This proves the
stated isotypic factorization and its normalization.}
}

\p{The algebraic tensor-to-map equivalence is formalized by
\leanref{Representation.Equiv.dualTensorHom}: its source order is
#{R^*\otimes\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}}, so first swap our two
factors. It requires finite-dimensional #{R}, with no dimension
restriction on #{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}}.
The fixed-map step is formalized by
\leanref{Representation.invariantsEquivIntertwiningMap} for the
conjugation action on linear maps. The Hilbert–Schmidt isometry and
#{\sqrt d} normalization are the direct arguments above. The earlier
[real isotypic projectors](fdip-004V) and
[multiplicity matrices](fdip-004Z) concern whole representation
components; a projector onto such a component does not by itself
impose the charged tensor constraint.}

\p{For reducible charge data, let #{\lambda} run over the
equivalence classes of irreducible continuous unitary
representations of #{K}, and write
#{M_\lambda=\operatorname{Hom}_K(R_\lambda,R)}.
Evaluation #{v\otimes\alpha\mapsto\alpha(v)} is a linear
isomorphism of representations
##{R\cong\bigoplus_\lambda R_\lambda\otimes M_\lambda,}
with #{K} acting on the first factor and trivially on
#{M_\lambda}. The charged identification, applied summandwise,
then gives a vector-space isomorphism
##{\mathcal M_R\cong
 \bigoplus_\lambda
 \operatorname{Hom}(M_\lambda,\mathcal M_{R_\lambda}).}
It is not claimed to be unitary. If these spaces are finite
dimensional and #{m_\lambda=\dim\mathcal M_{R_\lambda}}, then
#{\dim\mathcal M_R=\sum_\lambda(\dim M_\lambda)\,m_\lambda}.
\ref{fdip-00MA} recovers the coordinate form on
#{\mathbb C^{n_\lambda}}. For the trivial one-dimensional charge,
the construction recovers the [neutral space](fdip-008A).}

\transclude{fdip-00MA}
